.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt SBASE 7
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm sbase
.Nd the base userland's sbase ports, and their licence
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Nine commands under
.Pa /bin
are C programs from sbase 0.1, the suckless project's Unix tools (MIT
licence), built for ABSD with the platform's C kit
.Pq Nm absd-cc , No Picolibc and the POSIX adapter :
.Xr cmp 1 ,
.Xr ed 1 ,
.Xr find 1 ,
.Xr grep 1 ,
.Xr od 1 ,
.Xr printf 1 ,
.Xr sed 1 ,
.Xr sort 1 ,
and
.Xr test 1 .
They are bounded ports, not a promise of a Unix environment: where a tool
needs something ABSD does not provide, it was patched to refuse that
feature with a message and a status, and each page lists those features
under
.Sx UNSUPPORTED ON ABSD .
No kernel or kit semantics were added for them.
.Pp
None of them shares a name with a native command, a ported Rust program,
or a shell builtin.
Commands deliberately not provided
.Po Nm kill , Nm chmod , Nm chown , Nm touch , Nm ln ;
see
.Xr intro 1
.Pc
were not ported, although sbase has them.
.Pp
Each program is read whole through the file server before it starts
(about 170 to 250 KB each).
Messages begin with the name the program was run as, and the error
texts are Picolibc's.
Regular expressions
.Pq Xr grep 1 , Xr sed 1 , Xr ed 1
are Picolibc's, in the C locale only.
.Sh QUALIFICATION
The programs pass the C kit's ELF gate, build twice to the same bytes, and
resolve every name; their expected outputs are pinned on the build host.
They have not yet been qualified as ABSD processes: what the pages say
about behaviour on ABSD is predicted from the C interface and the patches
until the guest run confirms it.
The platform's
.Pa userland/README.md
records the build, the patches, and the cases.
